## 3. Multi-marker / last-end-marker claim (task 4)

Constructs (all on copies of B1; `sha256` computed over the actual file prefix):

| case | construction | old rc | new rc | result |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| multi0 | append a second self-consistent end marker (LF), no forged content | **0** | **0** | **ACCEPTED by both** — `INTEGRITY OK: sealed at line 31, sha cddcb972…` |
| multi1 | append `FORGED-LINE\n` + second self-consistent marker (LF) | **0** | **0** | **ACCEPTED by both** — `sealed at line 32, sha a48a9d23…` |
| multi2 | same as multi1 but second marker **without** trailing LF | **0** | **0** | **ACCEPTED by both** |
| multi-badsha | second marker whose sha covers bytes that are not in the file (my initially flawed construction — the `FORGED-LINE` was never written) | **1** | **1** | both reject: `prefix sha mismatch` (bonus negative control: a *malformed* second marker is still caught) |
| multi3 | tamper 1 byte of a sealed `.stdout`, re-seal marker only (manifest not updated) | **1** | **1** | both reject: `stage evidence hash changed` (partial re-seal does not hide stage tampering) |
| multi3b | tamper `.stdout` byte + rewrite the manifest hash in the log + fresh self-consistent marker (full re-seal) | **0** | **0** | **ACCEPTED by both**; resulting sha `80344e6c…45d5` **exactly equals** the author's BOUNDARY 1 reproduction — independent byte-level reproduction of the author's no-key demonstration |

**Reviewer claim verified: the checker takes the last end marker (`grep -abo … | tail -n 1`), so
appending a second self-consistent marker is accepted (rc=0) by the new checker — and also by the
old checker (0/0 on every multi case).**

**Classification: the same "no key" limitation, not a distinct defect class introduced by the fix.**
Root cause in all cases is the unkeyed seal: anyone who can write the log can compute a new valid
marker over any prefix. The last-marker anchoring (`tail -n 1`) is the *mechanism* that lets the
attack be **append-only** (log grows, original seal untouched) instead of requiring a rewrite. It
is inherited from the old checker unchanged. I demonstrated the anchoring choice matters: a
first-match variant (only `tail -n 1` → `head -n 1` on the marker grep, `evidence/06-firstmatch-demo.txt`)
still passes both pristine bases (rc=0) but **rejects multi0/1/2 (rc=1, "content after the end
marker")**. So: (a) the claim is real; (b) it is a sharper instance of the documented no-key
limitation, not a new defect class; (c) the author's report documents the root cause (REPORT §5.1:
「无密钥，可无损重封 … 只能防无意损坏，不防有意重封」) but does not name the append-only
sub-case by name — the Wiki note **TCN-99** fills that gap ("可追加式重封", plus the minimal
tightening: assert exactly one marker line, or an out-of-log anchor/key). Verdict: documented
accurately at the root-cause level; the append sub-case should be added to the REPORT wording.

