| 6 B (no LF) | B2 | `6 … byte 3173 of 3179` | `6 byte(s) … byte 3173 of 3179` | **no** |
| 19 B (no LF) | B1 | `19 … byte 2622 of 2641` | `19 byte(s) … byte 2622 of 2641` | **no** |
| 22 B (LF) | B1 / B2 | `22 … byte 2622 of 2644` / `byte 3173 of 3195` | both as expected | **no** |
| 200000 B (no LF) | B1 | `200000 … byte 2622 of 202622` | `200000 byte(s) … byte 2622 of 202622` | **no** |
| 6 B live attempt `…T203259-3291371` (3221 B) | live tree | `6 … byte 3215 of 3221` (independent: marker_off=3124, line 91 B) | `6 byte(s) past the marker line, byte 3215 of 3221` | **no** |

Caveat (verified, E2): when the marker line itself has **no** terminating LF, appended bytes are
absorbed into "the marker line" by `head -n 1`, so the byte gate does not fire and detection
happens in the sha layer instead. The patch's comment "so bytes appended without a trailing
newline are caught as well" is therefore true **via the sha layer** in that sub-case, not via the
byte assertion. No off-by-one was found at any size tested (1, 6, 19, 22, 200000 B, two bases,
one real tampered attempt).

